WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
So today we're looking at the pig heart

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.000
and the first thing to do is be able to

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
tell the anterior surface from the

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.000
posterior

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.000
surface. You can kind of tell there's a

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
jumble of vessels on the back, but what I

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.000
always use to help me make sure I'm

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
looking at the front is I find the

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
pulmonary trunk, which has been dissected

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.000
on this one, but you can see it has a

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.000
slightly different coloration to it, and

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.000
then that will orient me that, okay, this

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
is the front of the pig heart. This is

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
the pulmonary trunk, therefore this must

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
be the right ventricle, and then this

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
over here must be the left ventricle.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Dividing the left and right ventricle,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.000
you can see a sulcus right here. It's a

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
little groove. We call this the

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
interventricular sulcus, and this is

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
where the left anterior descending

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
artery flows, and if someone has a heart

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.000
attack, the probability is greatest that

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
this is the vessel that is indeed

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.000
clogged. Okay, so then we'll look up a

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
little bit higher and we can find the

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.000
auricles, and auricle just means ear. And so

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000
here is one on this side. This is the

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.000
left ventricle, so this must be the left

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.000
auricle. You can see it's kind of flappy,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.000
like an ear, and the auricle is the part

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.000
of the atrium that flaps out like this.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.000
So there's actually more space inside of

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.000
the atrium, but the part of it that forms

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
this ear-like shape is called the auricle.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.000
So if it's pinned from the outside, you

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
say auricle. If the chamber is

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.000
pinned, then you say atrium. And then you

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000
can find the other flap over on this

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
side, so this would be, since this is the

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
right ventricle, then up here this must

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.000
be the right

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.000
auricle. The next vessel to find would be

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.000
the aorta. So if you, this is the

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.000
pulmonary trunk, and you look right

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.000
behind it, the large vessel you see

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
coming up should be the aorta, and you

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.000
can see how strong and muscular that is.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.000
So if you're looking at it from on top,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.000
there's the pulmonary trunk going down

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000
to the right ventricle, and here's the

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.000
aorta going down behind the pulmonary

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
trunk to the left ventricle. Okay, now

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.000
we'll look at the posterior surface and

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
find a few important structures here. If

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.000
you think about blood coming into the

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.000
heart, it has to come through the

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.000
superior and inferior vena cava. Once you've

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
turned the heart on its backside, now

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.000
you're actually looking at the right

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.000
ventricle here, and this would be the

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.000
right auricle. So I'm going to look for

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.000
the superior and inferior vena cava dumping

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.000
into this area, and sure enough, although

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.000
it has been cut on this one a little bit,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.000
this is the superior and inferior

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.000
vena cava. If

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.000
you want to double check your work, you can

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.000
stick your finger in there and see how

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.000
my finger is now into the right auricle

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.000
and into the inside of the right

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
atrium. Again, this is cut right here. It's

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.000
been dissected. Normally this would be a

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.000
tube. So the superior vena cava enters right

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
here, and then the inferior vena cava enters

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.000
right here. Never be afraid to stick your

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.000
fingers in there to see where it goes,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.000
and then you'll know what vessel it is.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.000
Okay, so that's how blood gets into the

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
right atrium, and then it goes down to

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.000
the right ventricle, and then it goes up

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.000
and out the pulmonary trunk to the lungs.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Then it comes back to the left side of

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.000
the heart. So since we're looking at the

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.000
back side of the heart, this would be the

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.000
left ventricle, which would make this the

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.000
left auricle up here. So if you want to

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.000
find a path going in to the left atrium,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.000
you would look right next to there and

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.000
see, and sure enough, this large opening

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.000
goes right into the left atrium. And what

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.000
this large opening is, is pulmonary

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.000
veins that have come together. So you had

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.000
two coming from over here and two coming

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.000
from this lung, and then those two, this

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.000
will be the left lung, the right lung,

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.000
and then those pulmonary veins dump into

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.000
the left atrium.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Okay, so now we're ready to open

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.000
the heart up and look at it. So I'm going

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.000
to go from the

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.000
front, and this has been dissected to

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.000
split it right down the middle, so you

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.000
can open it up like a book, and now we'll

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.000
look at some structures on the inside.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
The first thing to notice is a large

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.000
piece of muscle, myocardium, that

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.000
separates the right ventricle from the

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.000
left ventricle. Notice that the right

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.000
ventricle has a thinner muscular wall

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.000
than the left ventricle, and that makes

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.000
sense because the left ventricle has to

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.000
pump blood out the aorta to the

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.000
entire body. The right ventricle only has

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.000
to pump it up to the lungs, which are

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.000
right next door.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.000
So you've got left atrium and left

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.000
ventricle, and the ventricles and

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.000
the atria are separated by the AV valves,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.000
and they're held down by these, these are the

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.000
chordae tendineae, those strands right there.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.000
And then here is one of the flaps of the

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.000
bicuspid, and here is the other flap of

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.000
the bicuspid. That's how it gets its name.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.000
Whereas if you look on the other side,

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.000
this is the right atrium, and then this

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.000
is the right AV valve, and it has three

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.000
flaps to it, so it's often called the

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.000
tricuspid, but it's also all right to say

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.000
right AV valve, left AV valve.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.000
Then here's the chordae tendineae again. The

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.000
chordae tendineae are held tightly down to the

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.000
bottom of the ventricles by these kind

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.000
of carrot-shaped muscles called

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.000
papillary

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.000
muscles. So papillary muscles, chordae tendineae,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.000
AV

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.000
valve. Now the next thing to find are the

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.000
semilunar valves. So this is the

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.000
aorta, and you can see that the

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:02.000
semilunar valve right here has

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.000
been cut. So blood goes into the left atrium, left

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.000
AV valve, up through the aortic semilunar

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.000
valve, and then to the whole

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.000
body.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.000
Okay, and then the pulmonary trunk

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:20.000
also has a semilunar valve, and you can

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:26.000
see it just by peeking right down in

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.000
there. Okay.
